        "predicate": "summary",
        "object": "The UK has abandoned projects worth tens of millions of pounds that were meant to help protect Congo rainforests and support local people. Together, these initiatives would have made up around half of the Â£200m that the UK pledged to support conservation in the Congo basin â€“ the worldâ€™s second-largest rainforest. When it hosted COP26 in Glasgow, the UK led a new initiative to end forest loss, which included a collective pledge by 12 donors of â€œat leastâ€ $1.5bn (Â£1.1bn) for Congo rainforest nations by 2025. Development minister Jenny Chapman revealed last week that, as of 2024, the UK had only provided Â£39.8m towards this goal. Alongside the US and much of Europe, the UK has significantly cut its aid budget in recent years, leading to much of its Congo rainforest spending being cancelled or reappraised. The government says it still plans to â€œ prioritise â€ rainforest regions, including the Congo basin, but civil society groups and MPs are concerned about the lack of â€œring-fencedâ€ forest funding in the UKâ€™s new aid strategy. COP pledge At COP26, the UK â€“ led by then prime minister Boris Johnson â€“ launched the â€œ Glasgow leadersâ€™ declaration â€, with a goal to â€œhalt and reverse forest lossâ€ by 2030. This was backed by more than 140 nations. The UK also made various funding pledges, including Â£200m to protect the Congo basin, Â£350m for tropical forests in Indonesia and â€œ up to Â£300m â€ for the Amazon. These commitments target the worldâ€™s three largest rainforests, all of which face major forest loss due to threats such as agriculture, logging and climate change .&nbsp; The Congo basin is the planetâ€™s largest forested carbon sink. Yet, its six host nations are among the poorest in the world and face signifƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;35DnH81bhkb27y81t17BXsnONQrzPa3mC7YZYCfkX_Mƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefE