        "predicate": "summary",
        "object": "<p>Evil Empire creatives explain how it is playing to todayâ€™s â€˜metroidvaniasâ€™ and honouring the originalâ€™s legacy with much fresh slaying to be done</p><p></p><p>Since the last Castlevania game hit the shelves (2014â€™s<a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2014/mar/02/castlevania-lords-of-shadow-2-dracula-sequel\"> Castlevania: Lords of Shadow 2</a>), Konamiâ€™s dormant series has unexpectedly spawned a hit genre. With an entire generation raised on â€œmetroidvaniasâ€ â€“ a portmanteau of Metroid and Castlevania â€“ millions of players have only ever seen the games <em>inspired by </em>Konamiâ€™s seminal games. Now with Belmontâ€™s Curse, launching in October, Castlevania is finally dashing back to console, where Konami hopes to reclaim its side-scroller throne.</p><p>Set 23 years after the events of 1989â€™s Castlevania 3 â€“ the same setting as the hit Netflix show â€“ Belmontâ€™s Curse shakes off the seriesâ€™ 3D ambitions and takes the Belmonts back to basics. Dispatching players to the demon-infested streets of 1499 Paris, youâ€™re placed in the tattered boots of Trevor Belmontâ€™s daughter, Rose. As a bishop pleads with the Belmonts to rid Paris of the ancient evil besetting the city, Rose heads into the sewers, longsword in hand, and her demon-slaying adventure begins.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/games/2026/jul/17/castlevania-belmonts-curse-komani-classic-paris-joan-of-arc-is-a-boss\">Continue reading...</a>"
