        "predicate": "summary",
        "object": "<p>A sinister new tablet threatens the honest-to-goodness toysâ€™ existence, but Buzz, Woody and Jessieâ€™s big tech moral battle feels compromised</p><p>The fifth episode of the Toy Story franchise is as slick and smooth as you like, as glitchless as Toy Story 6 or Toy Story 7 might be â€¦ or will be. As a piece of family-entertainment content it has the unblemished sheen of a brand new smartphone. But at heart, it has gone dead. For all the intensive, high-energy creative work that has clearly gone into this filmâ€™s every frame, the jeopardy, the novelty, the ideas and the passion are lacking; the crucial Toy Story theme of mortality feels underpowered, and the film even calamitously loses its nerve with its own big idea â€“ those squeamish about spoilers had better look away now â€“ the sinister way addictive tech devices are undermining the imaginative play that kids once had with honest-to-goodness toys.</p><p>Here a creepy tablet device called Lilypad (voiced by Greta Lee) enters the childrenâ€™s world, but ultimately proves to be capable of sentimental self-sacrificial heroism when it comes to their mental health. Really? At least Lots-oâ€™-Hugginâ€™ Bear, the villain from TS3, had the courage of his evil convictions.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/film/2026/jun/16/toy-story-5-review-pixar-franchise-needs-new-batteries\">Continue reading...</a>"
      },
